23:03:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s a great question that touches on both the achievements and the formidable limits of nuclear physics. In fact, we *do* use particle accelerators to create new elements—every element heavier than uranium (93 to 118) was discovered this way. But when we say “new elements we know the properties of,” you’re likely referring to the idea that, if we can predict a stable or useful superheavy element on paper, we should be able to simply make it. The reality is that producing such an element, even one atom at a time, is enormously difficult, and making a usable quantity is currently impossible. Here’s why. ### 1. The nuclear reaction is incredibly improbable To make a new element, we smash a beam of ionised atoms (the projectile) into a thin foil of another element (the target). For the heaviest elements, the probability that the two nuclei fuse into a compound nucleus—rather than just grazing each other, breaking apart, or undergoing fission—is measured in **femtobarns** (10⁻³⁹ cm²). That’s like trying to hit a needle on Mars with another needle fired from Earth, and then hoping they stick together. Even with the most intense beams available, we often need months of continuous bombardment to detect a single atom of a new element. Once that atom is made, it must be separated and identified within seconds before it decays. ### 2. The new atoms decay almost instantly Most superheavy elements we can currently produce are extremely neutron‑deficient. They have half‑lives in the millisecond to second range, and they undergo fission or alpha decay before we can accumulate more than a handful of atoms. No chemical test or practical application is possible because the sample literally vanishes while you are trying to measure it. ### 3. We can’t reach the theoretical “island of stability” Theoretical models predict that certain superheavy nuclei with just the right numbers of protons and neutrons (e.g., around 114‑126 protons and 184 neutrons) might be relatively stable—half‑lives perhaps reaching minutes, days, or longer. The problem is that the target and projectile combinations we have on hand (stable or near‑stable isotopes) always produce neutron‑poor compound nuclei. To make a nucleus with 184 neutrons, we would need extremely neutron‑rich beams or targets that don’t exist in sufficient intensity. Current accelerator technology cannot deliver a beam of, say, calcium‑60 (too unstable), and no practical target material possesses enough excess neutrons. So even though theory tells us *what* properties a longer‑lived superheavy element would have, we are physically unable to approach that region of the nuclear chart with today’s fusion reactions. ### 4. Knowing the properties on paper isn’t enough When scientists say they “know the properties” of an undiscovered element, they are relying on theoretical calculations that are far from perfect. The predictions for half‑lives, decay modes, and chemical behaviour can vary by orders of magnitude between different models. The only way to confirm them is to produce the element, which brings us back to the barriers described above. And even if a specific isotope is predicted to have a day‑long half‑life, we might never be able to synthesize that exact isotope because no feasible reaction can give it the correct neutron number. ### 5. Scaling up to visible amounts is out of reach Producing one or two atoms is a triumph; making a microgram (around 10¹⁶ atoms) is unimaginable with current technology. The production rate for the heaviest elements is roughly one atom per month. To obtain a visible speck, you would need to run the accelerator for times far exceeding the age of the universe, while simultaneously dealing with the fact that the atoms decay before you can collect them. So even if a stable superheavy were finally reached, we couldn’t manufacture it in any practical quantity for electronics, medicine, or materials science. ### In short
